    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d163">

                <pb facs="0308" n="223" sameAs="frus:frus1919v02/pg223"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">860d.01/48: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Davis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >London</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-12-08T17:00:00+00:00"><hi rendition="#italic">December 8,
                                1919, 5 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received
                            December 8, 3.30 p.m.</hi>]</dateline></opener>
                <p>3504. Your 6247, December 6, 4 [<hi rendition="#italic">3</hi>] p.m., regarding
                    recognition of Finland. Announcement made in House of Commons referred to in my
                    2000, May 7, 5 p.m.,<anchor n="10" xml:id="d163-f1"/> was as follows: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“His Majesty’s Government have now recognized the independence of Finland
                            and the <hi rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> Finnish Government.”</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <p>Foreign Office informs me that this was a loose statement but intended to convey
                        <hi rendition="#italic">de jure</hi> recognition of the hitherto <hi
                        rendition="#italic">de facto</hi> Government; <pb facs="0309" n="224"
                        xml:id="pg224"/> that a telegram of recognition was despatched by the
                    British Peace Delegation at Paris on May 4th or 5th; and that since that time
                    British Chargé d’Affaires have [<hi rendition="#italic">has?</hi>] functioned in
                    Finland until the arrival there a fortnight ago of Lord Acton, the British
                    Minister.</p>
                <p>Ossian Donner presented his credentials as Finnish Minister here on November
                    14.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Davis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
